          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The flower directly avows a present loving emotional state toward the little prince. The surrounding statement presents his prior ignorance as resulting from her own failure to communicate it, reinforcing the expression as a corrective disclosure.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression avows love but does not itself undertake a future action, obligation, or continuing relational commitment. The nearby wishes and instructions concern his departure and should not be transferred onto the love avowal. No undertaking by the flower through this expression, and therefore no uptake of one, is represented.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The avowal occurs within a composed apology and explanation rather than as a reflex-like affective discharge. The narration explicitly characterizes the flower's manner as calm, even though she later conceals tears.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Its core force is adequately represented as a truth-presenting avowal of love. Its contextual role as a belated correction of the little prince's ignorance does not constitute a separate core mode beyond T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince's departure, after apologizing for her prior behavior, the flower calmly discloses that she loves him and says that her failure to show it was her fault. He is surprised by her gentleness but gives no verbal response to the avowal in the supplied scene.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no distinct undertaking. The flower's adjacent directives and wish that he be happy are separate speech acts, not terms of a promise embedded in “je t'aime.” Consequently, no acceptance or rejection of an undertaking is shown; the little prince remains present and surprised, then continues preparing to leave.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognizable as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince. The classification relies only on the provided blocks.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports a sincere reading through the flower's apology, concealed crying, and the narration's reference to her tenderness, but actual inner feeling cannot be independently verified. The supplied scene does not show a verbal response to the avowal or establish any explicit promise arising from it.\"}"
